WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:12.530
<v Ira Glass>Mae's six years old, in first grade. And she's the kind of forthright kid who, when we sit down for an interview, before I can get to any of the things that I want to ask her, she launches into a few get-to-know-you questions of her own.
<v Ira Glass>Mae今年六岁，上一年级。她是一个非常直率的孩子，当我们坐下来采访时，我还没来得及问我想问的问题，她就开始问我一些她想了解的问题了。

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.350
<v Ira Glass>Hi, Mae.
<v Ira Glass>嗨，Mae。

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.540
<v Mae>Uh, what's your favorite color?
<v Mae>嗯，你最喜欢的颜色是什么？

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.410
<v Ira Glass>We discuss favorite colors. And then she moves on to--
<v Ira Glass>我们讨论了最喜欢的颜色。然后她接着问——

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.190
<v Mae>What's your middle name?
<v Mae>你的中间名是什么？

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.770
<v Ira Glass>I'm glad you're asking me that. My middle name is--
<v Ira Glass>很高兴你问我这个。我的中间名是——

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.150
<v Ira Glass>We move on to last names. And soon, she's instructed me on the pronunciation of her last name.
<v Ira Glass>我们转而谈论姓氏。很快，她就开始教我怎么读她的姓了。

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.980
<v Mae>Can you say "Zmyou-jen-ski"? That's how you say it.
<v Mae>你会读“Zmyou-jen-ski”吗？那是正确的读法。

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.270
<v Ira Glass>"Zmeel-jensky."
<v Ira Glass>“Zmeel-jensky”。

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:38.990
<v Mae>[SNORTS] "Zmyou-jen-ski". Z-M-U-D-C-Z-Y-N-S-K-I.
<v Mae>[吸鼻涕声] “Zmyou-jen-ski”。Z-M-U-D-C-Z-Y-N-S-K-I。

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:46.710
<v Ira Glass>The story I was there to talk to Miss "Zmyou-jen-ski" about happened this fall, at the beginning of the school year. In St. Louis, where she lives, it was hot out.
<v Ira Glass>我这次去采访“Zmyou-jen-ski”小姐的故事发生在今年秋天，学年刚开始的时候。在她居住的圣路易斯，天气很热。

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:55.470
<v Mae>It's sort of a funny story. So my sister told me to wear long sleeves-- I mean, short sleeves, because it was, like, summer.
<v Mae>这算是一个有趣的故事。我姐姐让我穿长袖——我是说，短袖，因为当时就像夏天一样。

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.240
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.150
<v Mae>So-- and I wanted to wear long sleeves, and I just got mad.
<v Mae>所以——我很想穿长袖，我就生气了。

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:02.800
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.430
<v Mae>And I also said, stop bossing me around.
<v Mae>我还说，别对我指手画脚。

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:12.140
<v Johanna>Though she really didn't like it, and she has a very fiery temper.
<v Johanna>虽然她真的很不喜欢这样，而且她脾气非常火爆。

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:23.000
<v Ira Glass>This is her older sister, Johanna. Johanna's eight, in third grade. Again, Mae's six, first grade. And Johanna, as the older sister, is very aware of the things that make Mae upset.
<v Ira Glass>这是她的姐姐Johanna。Johanna八岁，上三年级。再说一次，Mae六岁，上一年级。作为姐姐，Johanna很清楚什么事情会让Mae心烦。

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.530
<v Johanna>Mostly, when I tell her what to do.
<v Johanna>通常是因为我告诉她该怎么做。

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.520
<v Ira Glass>Especially, she says, when it comes to what Mae is wearing.
<v Ira Glass>她说，尤其是在Mae穿什么衣服的问题上。

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:47.680
<v Johanna>Like, she thinks fashion disasters are, like, the worst thing ever to happen. So she gets really angry when I tell her what to wear. And yeah, Mae gets very angry a lot. And her face turns red, and yeah.
<v Johanna>比如，她觉得时尚灾难是有史以来最糟糕的事情。所以我告诉她穿什么时她会非常生气。是的，Mae经常非常生气。她的脸会变红，是的。

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.610
<v Ira Glass>Here's Mae.
<v Ira Glass>这是Mae。

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.620
<v Mae>I was, like, so frustrated. I had a big tantrum.
<v Mae>我当时，太沮丧了。我发了一顿大脾气。

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.080
<v Ira Glass>What do you do when you have a tantrum?
<v Ira Glass>你发脾气的时候会做什么？

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.020
<v Mae>I usually do this with my fist.
<v Mae>我通常会这样握紧拳头。

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.760
<v Ira Glass>You're scrunching your fist as tight as they can go.
<v Ira Glass>你把拳头攥得紧紧的。

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:06.900
<v Mae>Yeah. Mm-hmm. And I was, like, jumping and jumping and twitching my legs.
<v Mae>是的。嗯哼。我当时还在不停地蹦跳，双腿抽动。

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.550
<v Ira Glass>Meanwhile, Mae and Johanna's mom, Megan, was trying to get them both dressed and ready for school.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，Mae和Johanna的妈妈Megan正试图帮她们穿好衣服，准备去上学。

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:23.560
<v Megan>And I just remember she was screaming, because I said, OK, Mae, you got to calm down so you can still get ready and get out the door. And she was just screaming that the only way she could calm down was if she punched her sister in the face.
<v Megan>我只记得她在尖叫，因为我说，好了，Mae，你得冷静下来，这样才能整理好出发。她一直尖叫说，唯一能让她冷静下来的方法就是打她姐姐的脸。

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.090
<v Mae>I told my mom, that's the only way for me to calm down.
<v Mae>我告诉妈妈，那是让我冷静下来的唯一方法。

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:44.680
<v Megan>And I said, well, that's not an option. So can you think of some other way to calm down? And then, I had to get ready, so I just left, and she was still kind of making quite a bit of noise. And then, when I came back, kind of miraculously, the tears had dried. She was smiling. Johanna was smiling.
<v Megan>我说，那是不可能的。所以你能想想其他冷静下来的方法吗？然后，我得准备一下，我就先走了，她当时还在闹腾。等我回来时，奇迹般地，眼泪干了。她在笑。Johanna也在笑。

00:02:44.680 --> 00:03:09.540
<v Ira Glass>What had happened? How did these two little kids work this out so quickly? Well, Mae and Johanna explained, it went down like this. Mae marched herself to Johanna's room and banged on the door. Johanna opened the door. And Mae has been working on not giving in to her anger, not taking the low road, but instead, using words, saying what she wants, which she did now.
<v Ira Glass>发生了什么？这两个小家伙是怎么这么快解决问题的？嗯，Mae和Johanna解释说，事情是这样的。Mae走到Johanna的房间门口，用力敲门。Johanna打开了门。Mae一直在努力控制自己的脾气，不走极端，而是用言语表达自己的需求，她刚才就是这么做的。

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:16.850
<v Mae>I said, I want to punch you in the face, and that's the only way I can calm down.
<v Mae>我说，我想打你的脸，那是唯一能让我冷静下来的方法。

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.190
<v Ira Glass>And then, Johanna, what did you say?
<v Ira Glass>然后，Johanna，你说了什么？

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.110
<v Johanna>Um, I want to kind of think about that.
<v Johanna>嗯，我想考虑一下。

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.780
<v Johanna>[IRA LAUGHS]
<v Johanna>[IRA笑]

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:33.140
<v Johanna>Um, you can punch me in the butt instead.
<v Johanna>嗯，你可以打我的屁股。

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.550
<v Ira Glass>This caught Mae off guard.
<v Ira Glass>这让Mae措手不及。

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:40.160
<v Mae>I was like, OK. And then, I just tried it.
<v Mae>我心想，好吧。然后，我就试了试。

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:48.390
<v Ira Glass>When you punched her butt, did you punch her like you were super mad? Or did you punch her kind of soft by then, because you were, sort of, half over it?
<v Ira Glass>当你打她屁股的时候，你是真的很生气吗？还是说你那时候已经没那么气了，因为气消了一半？

00:03:48.390 --> 00:04:05.490
<v Mae>I punched-- I punched it hard. Because, like-- so, like, to try to get all the anger out. Because if I punched it softly, it sort of-- it sort of only lets a couple pieces of the madness out of you.
<v Mae>我打——我用力打了。因为，就像——为了把所有的怒气都发泄出来。因为如果我轻轻地打，它只会——只会把体内的一点点怒火发泄出去。

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.180
<v Ira Glass>And did it work? Did you get all the anger out?
<v Ira Glass>有效吗？你把怒气都发泄出来了吗？

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.870
<v Mae>Yes, because I punched it super hard.
<v Mae>是的，因为我超级用力地打了。

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.930
<v Johanna>She definitely hit it very hard.
<v Johanna>她确实打得很重。

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.750
<v Ira Glass>How was that for you?
<v Ira Glass>那对你来说感觉怎么样？

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:30.710
<v Johanna>Um, I'm used to having a lot of siblings, and I get beat up by them all the time. So it didn't really hurt me.
<v Johanna>嗯，我习惯了有很多兄弟姐妹，我总是被他们揍。所以这对我来说其实不疼。

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.200
<v Ira Glass>Why did that work?
<v Ira Glass>为什么这样会有用？

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.120
<v Mae>I don't know. [CHUCKLES]
<v Mae>我不知道。[轻笑]

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:52.610
<v Ira Glass>It's funny. I wonder if it worked because she was sort of nice about it. Like, you came to her all mad, and then she didn't get mad back. Instead, she greeted your anger with niceness.
<v Ira Glass>这很有趣。我在想，是不是因为她态度比较好才起作用的？比如，你带着满腔怒火去找她，而她没有以牙还牙地发火。相反，她用友善回应了你的怒气。

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:59.200
<v Mae>I don't really know why she acted nice to me when I was frustrated.
<v Mae>我真的不知道为什么我在沮丧时她对我那么好。

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.950
<v Ira Glass>Have you ever heard of this phrase, "turn the other cheek"?
<v Ira Glass>你听说过“回敬另一边脸”这个短语吗？

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:03.880
<v Mae>No.
<v Mae>没有。

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:22.720
<v Ira Glass>It's something that I think Jesus said. He said that if somebody slaps you on your right cheek-- like, you know, slaps you on the cheek of your face-- he says, turn the other cheek, and offer the other cheek as well. Even if somebody's mad at you and does something unreasonable, you shouldn't get unreasonable yourself.
<v Ira Glass>我想这是耶稣说过的话。他说，如果有人打你的右脸——你知道的，就是打你的脸颊——他建议，要把另一边脸也转过去让他打。即使有人对你发火，做了不合理的事，你也不应该表现得不合理。

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.250
<v Mae>Probably.
<v Mae>也许吧。

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:31.870
<v Ira Glass>And in this case, your sister turned the other cheek, but it was her butt cheek that she turned.
<v Ira Glass>在这种情况下，你姐姐转过了另一边脸，但她转过来的是她的屁股。

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:37.420
<v Mae>[CHUCKLES] That's funny.
<v Mae>[轻笑] 那真好笑。

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:58.780
<v Ira Glass>But it wasn't just Johanna who led them to a quick, peaceful resolution. Both girls agree that if Mae hadn't asked for what she wanted at the door, if she just punched Johanna, Johanna would have punched back. It would have escalated. Johanna admits she's not always so calm and reasonable with Mae. But this time, it was clear how to handle her, and Johanna knew exactly what to do.
<v Ira Glass>但这不仅仅是Johanna引导她们快速和平解决问题。两个女孩都同意，如果Mae在门口没有说出她的想法，如果她只是打了Johanna，Johanna肯定会打回去。事情就会升级。Johanna承认她对Mae并不总是那么冷静和讲理。但这一次，处理她的方法很明确，Johanna完全知道该怎么做。

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.270
<v Johanna>And she got what she wanted, so I think that made her happy.
<v Johanna>而且她得到了她想要的，所以我认为这让她很开心。

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:07.650
<v Ira Glass>I think that's true. Yeah. Like, you didn't say no to her.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得是真的。是啊。你没有拒绝她。

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:22.510
<v Johanna>Yeah. I've been here for six-- I've been with her for six years of my life. I know her pretty well. I've known her for her whole life, so I get to watch a whole entire movie about her in my mind.
<v Johanna>是的。我在这里待了六——我和她在一起生活了六年。我很了解她。我从她出生起就认识她，所以我脑海里就像一直在看一部关于她的电影。

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.630
<v Ira Glass>And so you know what to do.
<v Ira Glass>所以你知道该怎么做。

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.880
<v Johanna>Yeah.
<v Johanna>是的。

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:52.150
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our show, in these noisy, aggressive times where the people rising to power in this world seem to come in hot, lots of fiery tempers and scrunched-up fists and twitchering legs, we pause for a moment to remember there is another path. We have stories of people turning the other cheek, pausing, and trying against all odds to see the good in each other, giving themselves over to the feeling in that old Otis Redding song--
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天在我们的节目中，在这个嘈杂、充满侵略性的时代，那些在这个世界上崛起的人似乎总是气势汹汹，带着火爆的脾气、紧握的拳头和抽动的双腿，我们暂停片刻，记住还有另一条道路。我们有一些故事，讲述人们如何回敬另一边脸，暂停一下，并在极尽所能的情况下看到彼此的优点，让自己沉浸在Otis Redding那首老歌的情感中——

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:52.480
<v Ira Glass>["TRY A
<v Ira Glass>["TRY A

00:06:52.480 --> 00:07:02.150
<v Ira Glass>LITTLE TENDERNESS" BY OTIS REDDING]
<v Ira Glass>LITTLE TENDERNESS" BY OTIS REDDING]

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:35.330
<v Ira Glass>Stay tuned for a hardboiled detective, for crows with a vendetta, for righteous spankings and non-spankings, for John Mulaney, and for a bus driver who thought he was God. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>敬请期待一位硬汉侦探，关于怀恨在心的乌鸦，关于正义的责打与非责打，关于John Mulaney，以及一位自以为是上帝的公交车司机。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:54.580
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, Act One. So there's an entire genre of movies and other stories, where tough guys who have been through it all and seen the worst in people decide they're going to soften, try a little tenderness, help somebody out who needs the help. This happens in old westerns, sci-fi films, spy stories.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》，第一幕。有一个完整的电影和故事流派，讲述那些经历过一切、看透了人性中最糟糕一面的硬汉们，决定要变得温柔一点，尝试一点点体贴，去帮助那些需要帮助的人。这种情况发生在老西部片、科幻电影、间谍故事中。

00:07:54.580 --> 00:08:12.830
<v Ira Glass>And there's a story like that on stage right now, on Broadway in New York. And I saw this a couple of weeks ago and thought, this story would be perfect for the radio. And the director and producers and writers said, yes. The show is called All In. And it stars John Mulaney, Richard Kind, Fred Armisen, and Chloe Fineman.
<v Ira Glass>现在纽约百老汇的舞台上也有这样一个故事。我几周前看了这部剧，觉得这个故事非常适合在广播里播出。导演、制片人和编剧都同意了。这部剧叫《All In》。由John Mulaney、Richard Kind、Fred Armisen和Chloe Fineman主演。

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:28.740
<v Ira Glass>And what they're doing is they're performing a set of short stories on stage by the writer Simon Rich, who's been on our show a few times with his stories. And let's get right to it. This story is one of the stories in their show. It's called "The Big Nap."
<v Ira Glass>他们正在舞台上表演作家Simon Rich的一系列短篇小说，他之前在我们的节目中讲过几次故事。我们直接进入主题吧。这个故事是他们剧中的一个短篇。它叫《大睡一觉》。

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.200
<v Ira Glass>[TYPEWRITER KEYS CLACKING, CARRIAGE RETURN]
<v Ira Glass>[打字机键声，换行声]

00:08:32.200 --> 00:09:09.150
<v Detective>Chapter one. I woke up just after dawn. It was a typical morning. My knees were scraped and bruised. My clothes were damp and soiled. And my teeth felt like someone had socked me in the jaw. I reached for the bottle I kept under my pillow and took a sloppy swig. The taste was foul, but it did the trick. Now, I could sit up and think. Now, I could start to figure out how to somehow face another goddamn day. I stared at my reflection in the mirror. I wasn't getting any younger. I was two years old.
<v Detective>第一章。我在黎明后醒来。这是一个典型的早晨。我的膝盖被擦伤和淤青了。我的衣服又湿又脏。我的牙齿感觉就像有人狠狠地揍了我的下巴。我伸手去拿放在枕头底下的瓶子，大口灌了一口。味道很难闻，但确实管用。现在，我可以坐起来思考了。现在，我可以开始琢磨如何去面对该死的又一天了。我盯着镜子里的倒影。我不再年轻了。我才两岁。

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.250
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:32.650
<v Detective>Soon, I would be three, unless I stayed two. I wasn't sure if you stayed the age you were or if that changed. I wasn't sure about a lot of things. All I knew was I was tired. Tired of this down and dirty life. Tired of trying to make some sense out of a world gone mad.
<v Detective>很快，我就要三岁了，除非我一直保持两岁。我不确定你是会保持现在的年龄，还是会有所改变。我不确定很多事情。我只知道我很累。厌倦了这个肮脏的生活。厌倦了试图从一个疯狂的世界里理出头绪。

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:49.190
<v Detective>The client was waiting for me in my nursery. I'd seen her around before. She'd come on the scene about a year ago, moving into the white bassinet down the hall. Some people called her "sweetheart." Others called her "pumpkin." But most people knew her by her full name, Baby Zoe.
<v Detective>客户正在我的托儿所里等我。我以前见过她。她大约一年前出现在现场，搬进了走廊尽头的白色摇篮里。有些人叫她“甜心”。其他人叫她“南瓜”。但大多数人叫她的全名，Zoe宝贝。

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:50.540
<v Detective>[DRAMATIC PIANO CHORD]
<v Detective>[戏剧性的钢琴和弦]

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:51.860
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:09:51.860 --> 00:10:12.780
<v Detective>Sure, she looked innocent enough with her big, wide eyes and her Princess Elsa onesie. But her past was murky. Some said she came from the hospital, but there was also a rumor she'd once lived inside Mommy's tummy. It didn't add up. Still, a job's a job. So what brings you here to see me?
<v Detective>当然，她看起来很天真，有着一双圆圆的大眼睛和她的Elsa公主连体衣。但她的过去很模糊。有人说她来自医院，但也有传言说她曾经住在妈咪的肚子里。这说不通。不过，工作就是工作。是什么风把你吹来找我了？

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.860
<v Baby Zoe>It's Moomoo. She's missing.
<v Baby Zoe>是Moomoo。她不见了。

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:15.880
<v Baby Zoe>[DRAMATIC PIANO CHORD]
<v Baby Zoe>[戏剧性的钢琴和弦]

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:24.840
<v Detective>I rolled my eyes. Moomoo went missing all the time. It was just the kind of unicorn she was. Maybe she's under your bassinet.
<v Detective>我翻了个白眼。Moomoo总是失踪。这就是那只独角兽的德行。也许她在你的摇篮下面。

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.370
<v Baby Zoe>I checked. She's not.
<v Baby Zoe>我找过了。不在那。

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:33.690
<v Detective>Her eyes filled with tears. I handed her a tissue, but she didn't know what a tissue was, so she put it in her mouth and tried to eat it.
<v Detective>她的眼睛里充满了泪水。我递给她一张纸巾，但她不知道纸巾是什么，所以她把它放进嘴里试图吃掉。

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:37.400
<v Baby Zoe>(MUFFLED) Please, Moomoo's all I have in this whole wide world!
<v Baby Zoe>（声音模糊）求你了，Moomoo是我这辈子拥有的全部！

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:45.530
<v Detective>Lost toys are small time. Why should I bust my ass trying to find some unicorn? Probably just turn up under the radiator.
<v Detective>丢失的玩具是小事。我为什么要拼了命去找什么独角兽？可能只是在暖气片底下。

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.550
<v Baby Zoe>Because I can pay you upfront.
<v Baby Zoe>因为我可以提前支付报酬。

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:55.800
<v Detective>I cocked my head doubtfully. What kind of scratch could a baby like Zoe come up with? She didn't have a piggy bank. She wasn't old enough to have pockets.
<v Detective>我怀疑地歪着头。像Zoe这样的婴儿能拿出什么钱来？她没有存钱罐。她还没有大到有口袋。

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.010
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.510
<v Detective>What have you got that's worth me getting up for?
<v Detective>你有什么值得我起身的东西吗？

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.390
<v Baby Zoe>Stickers.
<v Baby Zoe>贴纸。

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:09.180
<v Detective>I swallowed. Are they the, uh-- the fun kind?
<v Detective>我吞了口唾沫。是那种，呃——有趣的吗？

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.180
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:17.210
<v Baby Zoe>See for yourself.
<v Baby Zoe>你自己看看。

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:26.660
<v Detective>They were fun, all right. I'd never seen so many Batmans in all my life. How do I know these aren't hot? Where'd you even get them?
<v Detective>确实很有趣。我这辈子从来没见过这么多蝙蝠侠贴纸。我怎么知道这些不是赃物？你从哪搞来的？

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:36.360
<v Baby Zoe>I don't remember. Sometimes things are just in my hand. I also don't remember how I got into this room or what we're talking about.
<v Baby Zoe>我不记得了。有时候东西就在我手里。我也不记得我是怎么进这个房间的，或者我们在说什么。

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.060
<v Baby Zoe>[LAUGHTER]
<v Baby Zoe>[笑声]

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:42.150
<v Detective>We're talking about how I'm gonna find your Moomoo.
<v Detective>我们在说我该怎么找到你的Moomoo。

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:42.990
<v Baby Zoe>You mean it?
<v Baby Zoe>你说真的？

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:52.700
<v Detective>That's right, doll. I'm on the case. Chapter two. So this is the rug where you last saw your Moomoo?
<v Detective>没错，小宝贝。我正在调查。第二章。这就是你最后一次见到Moomoo的地毯吗？

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:54.830
<v Baby Zoe>Yes, someone must have taken her.
<v Baby Zoe>是的，肯定是有人拿走了她。

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.290
<v Detective>Have you seen any suspicious characters around lately?
<v Detective>你最近有没有看到什么可疑的人？

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:58.460
<v Baby Zoe>Not that I can remember.
<v Baby Zoe>我记得没有。

00:11:58.460 --> 00:11:59.210
<v Detective>Think hard.
<v Detective>好好想想。

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:05.870
<v Baby Zoe>Well, now that you mention it, an old lady appeared out of nowhere yesterday and replaced my parents.
<v Baby Zoe>嗯，既然你提到了，昨天有个老太太突然出现，取代了我的父母。

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:07.460
<v Detective>Interesting. Describe her.
<v Detective>有意思。描述一下她。

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:11.540
<v Baby Zoe>Gray hair, glasses, smells like yogurt.
<v Baby Zoe>灰头发，眼镜，闻起来像酸奶。

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:13.100
<v Detective>Large, wooden jewelry?
<v Detective>很大的木制首饰？

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:13.750
<v Baby Zoe>Yeah.
<v Baby Zoe>是的。

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:15.030
<v Detective>Sounds like Nana.
<v Detective>听起来像是Nana。

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:17.440
<v Detective>[DRAMATIC PIANO CHORD]
<v Detective>[戏剧性的钢琴和弦]

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.210
<v Baby Zoe>You know her?
<v Baby Zoe>你认识她？

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.620
<v Detective>I've had run-ins with her before.
<v Detective>我以前和她有过冲突。

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:22.600
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.560
<v Detective>She's from Florida.
<v Detective>她是来自佛罗里达的。

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.610
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:28.940
<v Baby Zoe>Where's Florida?
<v Baby Zoe>佛罗里达在哪里？

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:29.890
<v Detective>It's in the sky.
<v Detective>在天上。

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:30.700
<v Baby Zoe>Really?
<v Baby Zoe>真的吗？

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:31.610
<v Detective>Yeah.
<v Detective>是的。

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:32.660
<v Baby Zoe>How do you get there?
<v Baby Zoe>怎么去那里？

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:37.400
<v Detective>You get in an airplane, and you watch cartoons. And when you wake up, you're in her house, and there's a turkey.
<v Detective>你坐上一架飞机，看动画片。当你醒来时，你就已经在她家了，还有火鸡。

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.310
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.530
<v Baby Zoe>If Nana's from Florida, what's she doing here?
<v Baby Zoe>如果Nana来自佛罗里达，她在这里干什么？

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:58.750
<v Detective>That's what I'm gonna find out. Chapter three. Nana was elusive. Sometimes she was lying in a recliner. Sometimes she was small inside a phone.
<v Detective>这就是我要查出来的。第三章。Nana神出鬼没。有时她躺在躺椅上。有时她变小钻进手机里。

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:03.780
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:24.520
<v Detective>If I was going to track her down, I needed good old-fashioned shoe leather. First, I tried the playroom. Then, I tried the room that has the chairs. I'd been at it for over 10 seconds when I finally caught a break in the case. Nana picked me up and started carrying me. Before long, she was making a phone call.
<v Detective>如果我要追踪她，我得靠老派的方式。首先，我试了游戏室。然后，我试了那个有椅子的房间。我才查了不到10秒钟，案情就有了突破。Nana抱起了我，开始带着我走。没过多久，她就在打电话。

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:32.200
<v Nana>How's the Airbnb? Are you both wearing sunscreen? Listen, I can't figure out the Bosch.
<v Nana>Airbnb怎么样？你们两个都涂防晒霜了吗？听着，我搞不定那个博世洗衣机。

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:39.430
<v Detective>It was impossible to follow. Like all grownups, Nana spoke in a secret code designed to keep her operations hidden.
<v Detective>这根本没法追踪。像所有成年人一样，Nana用的是一种秘密代码，专门用来隐藏她的行踪。

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:46.760
<v Nana>What does Smart Cycle mean? Warm or cold? Hold on. I'm putting you on speaker.
<v Nana>“智能循环”是什么意思？温水还是冷水？等等。我开免提。

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:52.110
<v Detective>And that's when a familiar voice rumbled out of Nana's phone.
<v Detective>就在这时，一个熟悉的声音从Nana的手机里传了出来。

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.450
<v Mama>We're driving to the beach. Just google it, OK?
<v Mama>我们正在开车去海滩。你自己去谷歌一下，好吗？

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:06.450
<v Detective>There was no mistaking it. The voice belonged to Mama. And that could only mean one thing. This one went all the way to the top.
<v Detective>绝对没错。这个声音属于妈咪。那只能意味着一件事。这件事牵扯到了幕后大老板。

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:07.550
<v Detective>[DRAMATIC PIANO CHORD]
<v Detective>[戏剧性的钢琴和弦]

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.650
<v Detective>Chapter four. I'm off the case.
<v Detective>第四章。我不干了。

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.960
<v Baby Zoe>What? But you said you'd help me.
<v Baby Zoe>什么？但你说你会帮我的。

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:19.250
<v Detective>That was before I knew the score. If Mama's mixed up in this, I don't want anything to do with it.
<v Detective>那是没了解情况之前。如果妈咪也卷进来了，我可不想沾边。

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:22.320
<v Baby Zoe>Ugh. What's wrong with Mama? I like Mama.
<v Baby Zoe>啊。妈咪怎么了？我喜欢妈咪。

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:30.390
<v Detective>We all like Mama. She smells nice and is warm. But that doesn't change the fact that she runs this whole goddamn town.
<v Detective>我们都喜欢妈咪。她闻起来很香，而且很温暖。但这并不能改变她统治整个该死小镇的事实。

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.450
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.470
<v Baby Zoe>What are you talking about?
<v Baby Zoe>你在说什么？

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:45.110
<v Detective>Look, you're just a kid, so I'll spell it out for you. Mama's the big boss around here. The doctor, the dentist, the Gymboree instructor. They're all on the take.
<v Detective>听着，你只是个孩子，那我给你解释清楚。妈咪是这里的大老板。医生、牙医、金宝贝早教老师。他们都被收买了。

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.110
<v Baby Zoe>But I thought Dadda was in charge.
<v Baby Zoe>但我以为爸爸是负责的。

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.980
<v Detective>Ha, ha, ha, ha!
<v Detective>哈，哈，哈，哈！

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.100
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:14:51.100 --> 00:15:02.100
<v Detective>Dadda. Dadda's just a bagman! He wears a shiny watch. It's Mama's the one that's calling all the shots.
<v Detective>爸爸。爸爸只是个传声筒！他戴着一块闪亮的手表。妈咪才是说了算的人。

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.670
<v Baby Zoe>Well-- well, what does this have to do with Moomoo?
<v Baby Zoe>那——那这和Moomoo有什么关系？

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:11.990
<v Detective>Oh, don't you get it? This is bigger than Moomoo. Something's going down here, and I'm not hanging around long enough to find out what it is.
<v Detective>噢，你还不明白吗？这比Moomoo严重多了。有什么事情要发生了，我可不会留在这里等死。

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:24.310
<v Detective>I was halfway to my crib when the girl began to sob. It didn't take a detective to see she was frightened. Her eyes were bleary, her face pale and drawn, and at some point, she had pooped big in her pants.
<v Detective>我走到一半回婴儿床时，那个女孩开始抽泣。不需要什么侦探就能看出她被吓坏了。她眼神呆滞，脸色苍白憔悴，而且不知道什么时候她在裤子里拉了一大坨。

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:27.320
<v Baby Zoe>Please! You've got to help me!
<v Baby Zoe>求你了！你一定要帮帮我！

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:34.420
<v Detective>I got enough problems of my own. Why should I risk my hide to help some dame I barely even know?
<v Detective>我自己已经够多麻烦了。我为什么要冒着生命危险去帮一个我几乎不认识的女人？

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.870
<v Baby Zoe>Because you're the only one who can!
<v Baby Zoe>因为只有你能做到！

00:15:37.870 --> 00:16:12.370
<v Detective>Chapter five. I shuffled down the hall, trying to figure out why I'd gotten mixed up in this crazy case. Was it just for the Batmans? Or was it something else? There was something about that screwy kid. The world had done her dirty, but somehow it hadn't made us cynical. She still believed in justice. She still believed in hope. She still believed that objects disappeared when you put a surface in front of her, and then reappeared by magic when you took away that surface.
<v Detective>第五章。我拖着脚步走在走廊里，试图弄清楚为什么我会卷进这个疯狂的案子。难道只是为了蝙蝠贴纸吗？还是别的原因？那个古怪的孩子身上有些特别的地方。世界对她很不公平，但不知为何她并没有变得愤世嫉俗。她依然相信正义。她依然相信希望。她依然相信当你把一个平面放在她面前时物体就会消失，而当你移开平面时它们又会神奇地重新出现。

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:49.630
<v Detective>She even believed in me. No one ever had before. I decided to swing by the TV room. Classic grownup hangout. The kind of after-hours joint that doesn't get hoppin' till after bedtime. I slipped inside and got to work, opening drawers, taking things out, and then dropping them hard on the ground. It's an old detective tactic, a way to make sure that you touched everything and that everything got everywhere. I'd gone through most of the cabinets when the door burst open. It was Nana. I spotted a closet and sprinted inside, but it turned out to be a dead end.
<v Detective>她甚至相信我。从来没有人相信过我。我决定去电视室转转。那是典型的成年人聚集地。那种直到睡前才开始热闹的地方。我溜进去开始干活，打开抽屉，把东西拿出来，然后用力扔在地上。这是一种老派侦探战术，为了确保你碰过每一件东西，而且让所有东西到处都是。我刚检查完大部分橱柜，门就砰地打开了。是Nana。我发现了一个壁橱，冲了进去，但结果是个死胡同。

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:51.040
<v Nana>How about a nap?
<v Nana>睡个午觉怎么样？

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:56.830
<v Detective>I shook my head defiantly. If she thought I was going down that easy, she had another thing coming.
<v Detective>我叛逆地摇了摇头。如果她以为我这么容易就范，那她就错了。

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.930
<v Nana>How about some yummy medicine?
<v Nana>来点美味的药药怎么样？

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:17.700
<v Detective>I ate the medicine. I like to eat things that were yummy, and Nana had used that word when describing the medicine. I smiled as the sweet cherry capsule hit my tongue, but it was quickly followed by a different flavor. The bitter taste of betrayal.
<v Detective>我吃了药。我喜欢吃美味的东西，Nana在描述药物时用过那个词。当甜甜的樱桃药囊触碰到舌尖时，我微笑了，但很快换成了另一种味道。背叛的苦味。

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:25.240
<v Detective>Chapter six. It wasn't the first time I'd been drugged.
<v Detective>第六章。这不是我第一次被下药了。

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:29.700
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:17:29.700 --> 00:18:08.250
<v Detective>Still, you couldn't help but marvel at the grownups' depravity. They never did you dirty to your face. Haha, no. They preferred to stab you in the back. They said you could skip your vegetables and snuck them in your mashed potatoes. They said you could sleep in their bed, then they put you in your crib the second you fall asleep. They said you could have a present, and then brought you a potty with a bow on it and told you to poop in it in front of them. Like that's some kind of a gift!
<v Detective>尽管如此，你还是不得不惊叹成年人的卑劣。他们从不当面整你。哈哈，不。他们喜欢背后捅刀子。他们说你可以不吃蔬菜，然后偷偷把它们藏在土豆泥里。他们说你可以睡在他们的床上，然后等你一睡着就把你放进摇篮。他们说你可以收到一份礼物，然后带给你一个带蝴蝶结的马桶，让你当着他们的面拉屎。就像那是什么礼物一样！

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:13.350
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:26.110
<v Detective>I stared out the bars of my crib. The grownups had taken my power and my freedom. But that just meant I had nothing left to lose.
<v Detective>我盯着婴儿床的栏杆。大人们夺走了我的权力和自由。但这反倒意味着我没什么可失去的了。

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:28.170
<v Detective>Chapter seven!
<v Detective>第七章！

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:33.640
<v Baby Zoe>Where have you been? I looked everywhere. Inside a cup, inside a shoe--
<v Baby Zoe>你去哪了？我到处都找了。杯子里，鞋子里——

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:36.040
<v Detective>I can't fit in any of those places.
<v Detective>我装不进那些地方。

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:36.660
<v Baby Zoe>Why not?
<v Baby Zoe>为什么？

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:37.870
<v Detective>'Cause I'm too big!
<v Detective>因为我太大了！

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.040
<v Baby Zoe>But when you're far away, you look small.
<v Baby Zoe>但当你离得很远时，你看上去很小。

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:43.910
<v Detective>Objects look big when they're close to you and small when they're far from you.
<v Detective>物体离你近的时候看起来很大，远的时候看起来很小。

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:46.830
<v Baby Zoe>Oh, god! What's happening?
<v Baby Zoe>噢，天呐！发生什么事了？

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:53.230
<v Detective>Nana tried to kill me! She drugged me and left me to die inside my crib.
<v Detective>Nana想杀了我！她给我下了药，让我死在婴儿床里。

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:54.290
<v Baby Zoe>How did you escape?
<v Baby Zoe>你是怎么逃出来的？

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.320
<v Detective>I said Nana, up, and then Nana came and picked me up.
<v Detective>我说Nana，起来，然后Nana就过来把我抱起来了。

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:57.760
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:00.680
<v Baby Zoe>Why did she try to help you after trying to kill you?
<v Baby Zoe>为什么她试图杀你之后又来帮你？

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:02.540
<v Detective>Why do you think? She's insane.
<v Detective>你觉得呢？她疯了。

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:03.320
<v Baby Zoe>So what do we do?
<v Baby Zoe>那我们该怎么办？

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:08.870
<v Detective>We run. Let's run away together. Somewhere far, far away where we can make a brand new start. Maybe the den!
<v Detective>我们跑。我们一起逃跑吧。去一个很远很远的地方，在那里我们可以重新开始。也许是书房！

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:09.710
<v Baby Zoe>We'll never make it!
<v Baby Zoe>我们跑不掉的！

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:13.270
<v Detective>Anything's better than hanging around here like a couple of sitting ducks.
<v Detective>什么都比像待宰羔羊一样呆在这里强。

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:14.200
<v Baby Zoe>Look!
<v Baby Zoe>看！

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:20.150
<v Detective>She pointed across the room. Nana was headed for the stairs, carrying a clear plastic bag.
<v Detective>她指着房间对面。Nana正朝楼梯走去，手里提着一个透明塑料袋。

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:25.130
<v Baby Zoe>It's Moomoo! She's in there. Oh, god! Where's Nana taking her?!
<v Baby Zoe>是Moomoo！她在里面。噢，天呐！Nana要带她去哪？！

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.090
<v Detective>The garage.
<v Detective>车库。

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:26.780
<v Baby Zoe>What's that?
<v Baby Zoe>那是什么？

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:32.300
<v Detective>It's the car's bedroom, where it sleeps. Also, there's a big door that goes up and down and is a monster.
<v Detective>那是车的卧室，它睡觉的地方。而且，那里有个大门会上下移动，是个怪物。

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.720
<v Baby Zoe>Well, you can't go down there. You'll disappear.
<v Baby Zoe>嗯，你不能去那里。你会消失的。

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:42.580
<v Detective>I won't disappear. Objects are permanent. Even when you can't see them, they are still there.
<v Detective>我不会消失的。物体是永恒的。即使你看不到它们，它们依然在那里。

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:46.480
<v Baby Zoe>Oh, god! I can't lose you, too!
<v Baby Zoe>噢，天呐！我也不能失去你！

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:51.450
<v Detective>You won't. I promise.
<v Detective>你不会的。我保证。

00:19:51.450 --> 00:20:03.850
<v Detective>Chapter eight. I climbed down the stairs as silently as possible while also still counting out loud to myself, because that's the game I do when I climb stairs.
<v Detective>第八章。我悄无声息地爬下楼梯，同时还在大声数数，因为那是爬楼梯时我玩的游戏。

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:07.680
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:16.590
<v Detective>As my eyes adjusted to the darkness, I realized Nana was not alone. Mama and Dadda had returned.
<v Detective>当我的眼睛适应了黑暗，我意识到Nana并不孤单。妈咪和爸爸回来了。

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:17.980
<v Mama>What happened?
<v Mama>发生什么事了？

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:23.320
<v Nana>It's not my fault. It's the damn Bosch. Why does it have to have so many settings?
<v Nana>不怪我。是该死的博世洗衣机。为什么它有那么多设置？

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:56.460
<v Detective>I was starting to lose the thread, but then I saw something that made my blood run cold. It was Moomoo, just where Zoe had said she'd be in the plastic bag. There was only one problem. She'd been murdered. Her horn had been severed, and her hooves ripped clean off her body. Nana had taken her life, and now, Mama was back to run the coverup. I watched with disgust as Dadda obediently tossed Moomoo's corpse into the trash.
<v Detective>我开始理不清头绪，但随后我看到了让我血液凝固的一幕。那是Moomoo，正像Zoe说的那样在塑料袋里。只有一个问题。她被谋杀了。她的角被割掉了，蹄子从身体上被硬生生扯了下来。Nana夺走了她的生命，现在，妈咪回来了掩盖真相。我厌恶地看着爸爸顺从地把Moomoo的尸体扔进了垃圾桶。

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:00.340
<v Mama>Don't worry. She'll never know. [CYNICAL CHUCKLE]
<v Mama>别担心。她永远不会知道的。[愤世嫉俗的笑]

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:04.600
<v Mama>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mama>[笑声]

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:12.080
<v Mama>I followed the grownups up the stairs. I was almost at the top when I heard a disconcerting sound. Laughter!
<v Mama>我跟着大人们上楼。我快走到顶端时，听到了一声令人不安的声音。笑声！

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:16.490
<v Baby Zoe>(LAUGHING) Look! Mama found Moomoo!
<v Baby Zoe>（笑着）看！妈咪找到Moomoo了！

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:29.600
<v Detective>I broke out in a cold sweat as I watched Mama take a new unicorn out of a plastic case. Zoe. Zoe, listen to me. That's not the real Moomoo. The real Moomoo is dead.
<v Detective>当我看着妈咪从塑料盒子里拿出一个新的独角兽时，我惊出了一身冷汗。Zoe。Zoe，听着。那不是真正的Moomoo。真正的Moomoo已经死了。

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:31.230
<v Baby Zoe>But this looks like Moomoo.
<v Baby Zoe>但这个看起来像Moomoo啊。

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:37.880
<v Detective>No, I know. I can't explain it, but you gotta believe me. I saw them bury her body. I saw it with my own damn eyes.
<v Detective>不，我知道。我解释不清楚，但你必须相信我。我亲眼看着他们埋葬了她的尸体。我亲眼看到的。

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:42.110
<v Baby Zoe>But Mama said this was Moomoo. She used the word, "Moomoo," and pointed at it.
<v Baby Zoe>但妈咪说这就是Moomoo。她用了“Moomoo”这个词，还指着它。

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:49.670
<v Detective>I grabbed her by the shoulders. You can't trust Mama. You can't trust any of them. They're all in on this together! Don't buy into their lies!
<v Detective>我抓住她的肩膀。你不能相信妈咪。你不能相信他们中的任何一个。他们是一伙的！不要相信他们的谎言！

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:52.090
<v Detective>[ZOE YELLING]
<v Detective>[ZOE尖叫]

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:55.820
<v Mama>Easy, sweetie. Remember, we play gentle.
<v Mama>轻松点，亲爱的。记住，我们玩的时候要温柔。

00:21:55.820 --> 00:22:11.590
<v Detective>The grownups were closing in on me. I didn't have much time. Zoe, baby, they're going to nab me any second. But before they do, please, I need to hear you say something. I need to hear you say that you believe me. I need to hear you say that this isn't Moomoo.
<v Detective>大人们正在逼近我。我没多少时间了。Zoe，宝贝，他们随时会抓住我。但在那之前，求你了，我需要听你说句话。我需要听你说你相信我。我需要听你说这不是Moomoo。

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:17.680
<v Baby Zoe>But Mama said it was.
<v Baby Zoe>但妈咪说是。

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:34.120
<v Detective>Whew. I thought we had something. Something real. I suppose I've been a fool. I'll just take my Batmans and go.
<v Detective>呼。我原以为我们有什么共识。真实的东西。我想我真是个傻瓜。我还是带着我的蝙蝠侠贴纸走吧。

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:37.500
<v Mama>Uh-oh, looks like someone got into the Hanukkah drawer.
<v Mama>呃哦，看起来有人溜进了光明节抽屉。

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:42.160
<v Detective>I watched with silent rage as Mama snatched the stickers from my hand.
<v Detective>我愤怒地看着妈咪从我手里抢走了贴纸。

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:48.270
<v Mama>He's going through a phase, sneaking around, getting into everything. He thinks he's a little detective.
<v Mama>他正在经历一个阶段，到处溜达，什么都碰。他以为自己是个小侦探。

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:53.250
<v Mama>[GROWNUPS LAUGHING]
<v Mama>[大人们笑]

00:22:53.250 --> 00:23:06.700
<v Detective>The grownups laughed in my face. And as the sick coffee breath hit my nose, something snapped inside my brain. Before I could stop myself, I was lunging at them, arms flailing and legs kicking.
<v Detective>大人们嘲笑我的脸。当恶心的咖啡味扑鼻而来时，我脑子里有什么东西崩断了。没等我停下来，我就冲向他们，挥舞着手臂，又踢又闹。

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:10.260
<v Mama>Terrible twos! Ha, ha, ha!
<v Mama>可怕的两岁！哈，哈，哈！

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:20.410
<v Detective>I punched and spat as the grownups tried to restrain me. But I was determined not to break this time, no. This time, I wasn't going down without a fight!
<v Detective>大人们试图制服我时，我挥拳又吐。但我这次决心不屈服，不。这一次，我绝不会不战而败！

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:24.400
<v Mama>How about some yummy medicine?
<v Mama>来点美味的药药怎么样？

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:35.400
<v Mama>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mama>[笑声]

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:47.970
<v Detective>I woke hours later. My mind was so foggy, it took me a second to realize I wasn't there alone. Staring up at me from the rug was Baby Zoe. What the hell are you doing here?
<v Detective>几小时后我醒了。我的大脑一片混沌，花了一秒钟才意识到我不是独自一人。地毯上盯着我看的是Zoe宝贝。你在这干什么？

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:49.720
<v Baby Zoe>I brought this for you.
<v Baby Zoe>我给你带了这个。

00:23:49.720 --> 00:24:06.220
<v Detective>She slid something to me through the bars of my crib. I-- I couldn't make it out in the darkness, but I could feel. Soft cape, groovy hooves, fun horn. Oh, don't be crazy, kid. This Moomoo is all you have.
<v Detective>她穿过婴儿床的栏杆把东西递给我。我——我在黑暗中认不出它，但我能摸出来。柔软的斗篷，帅气的蹄子，有趣的角。噢，别傻了，孩子。这个Moomoo是你拥有的全部。

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:21.030
<v Baby Zoe>I want us to be square. Besides, it's not like it's the real Moomoo.
<v Baby Zoe>我想让我们扯平。再说，这又不是真正的Moomoo。

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.270
<v Detective>What are you saying?
<v Detective>你在说什么？

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:27.780
<v Baby Zoe>I'm saying I believe you. Anyways, see you around.
<v Baby Zoe>我是说我相信你。总之，后会有期。

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:39.480
<v Detective>She started to crawl away. She was halfway across the very small rug she was on when I said, wait, hey! Hold on now. This case isn't closed. A lot remains unsolved.
<v Detective>她开始爬开。当她爬到那块很小的地毯中间时，我说，等等，嘿！先别走。这个案子还没结。还有很多未解之谜。

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:40.380
<v Baby Zoe>Like what?
<v Baby Zoe>比如什么？

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:52.630
<v Detective>We still don't know why Mama and Dadda went away this weekend. Or where they went or what they did there. We don't know why they go to work or what work is, or why they both have glasses.
<v Detective>我们仍然不知道为什么妈咪和爸爸这个周末要离开。或者他们去了哪里，在那里做了什么。我们不知道他们为什么要上班，工作是什么，或者为什么他们都戴眼镜。

00:24:52.630 --> 00:25:08.810
<v Detective>We don't know their penis and vagina situation. Or why they take showers and not baths. We don't even know if Mama and Dadda are their real names.
<v Detective>我们不知道他们的阴茎和阴道情况。或者为什么他们洗澡而不是洗盆浴。我们甚至不知道妈咪和爸爸是不是他们的真名。

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:11.090
<v Baby Zoe>That's a lot to crack.
<v Baby Zoe>那可够难查的。

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:17.130
<v Detective>Sure, but I was thinking maybe it'd be easier if we cut a deal.
<v Detective>当然，但我在想如果我们达成协议会不会容易些。

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.250
<v Baby Zoe>What are you saying?
<v Baby Zoe>你在说什么？

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:23.330
<v Detective>I'm saying partners. You and me.
<v Detective>我是说合作。你和我。

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:27.450
<v Baby Zoe>Well, I don't have any experience. You'd have to train me.
<v Baby Zoe>嗯，我没有任何经验。你得训练我。

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:32.420
<v Detective>It won't take long. We can start right now. I handed her a crayon to take notes.
<v Detective>用不了多久。我们现在就可以开始。我递给她一支蜡笔来做记录。

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:33.830
<v Baby Zoe>Is this food?
<v Baby Zoe>这是吃的吗？

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.240
<v Detective>Sort of.
<v Detective>算是吧。

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:39.070
<v Detective>[LAUGHTER]
<v Detective>[笑声]

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:55.570
<v Baby Zoe>OK, I'm completely lost. I don't know where I am, and I forgot what time it is! I-- I don't know if I'm awake or if this is a dream! And also, I do not understand mirrors.
<v Baby Zoe>好的，我完全迷糊了。我不知道我在哪，我忘了现在是几点！我——我不知道我是醒着还是在做梦！而且，我完全不理解镜子。

00:25:55.570 --> 00:26:02.910
<v Detective>Don't worry, kiddo. We'll figure it all out. Together.
<v Detective>别担心，小家伙。我们会搞清楚一切的。一起。

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:08.740
<v Detective>[APPLAUSE]
<v Detective>[掌声]

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:38.570
<v Ira Glass>John Mulaney, Chloe Fineman, Richard Kind, and Fred Armisen. They're the stars of All In: Comedy About Love. But the cast is going to change soon and star other great people. The show was written by Simon Rich. It runs through February 16 on Broadway, New York City, at the Hudson Theater. Coming up-- how do you get a wild animal that hates your guts to turn the other cheek, especially if it doesn't have cheeks? That's in a minute on Chicago Public Radio-- when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>John Mulaney、Chloe Fineman、Richard Kind和Fred Armisen。他们是《All In: Comedy About Love》的主演。但演员阵容很快就会改变，会有其他伟大的演员加入。该剧由Simon Rich编剧。它将于2月16日在纽约百老汇的哈德森剧院上演。接下来——你如何让一只厌恶你的野生动物转过另一边脸，尤其是如果它根本没有脸颊？一分钟后在芝加哥公共广播电台——节目继续。

00:26:38.570 --> 00:27:04.260
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, "Try a Little Tenderness," we have stories of people putting aside aggressiveness and anger and disappointment in other human beings, and reaching out in kindness instead. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The Gladiator, starring Ruffled Crow. Aviva DeKornfeld brings us this story about a person who tried to be considerate and where it got him.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。今天我们的节目主题是“尝试一点点温柔”，我们讲述了人们抛开侵略性、愤怒和对他人的失望，转而伸出友善之手的故事。我们已经进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕，《角斗士》，由愤怒的乌鸦主演。Aviva DeKornfeld给我们带来了这个故事，关于一个试图体贴他人却得到这种下场的人。

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:26.790
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>This story starts with a little tenderness. Alan Martin's a real nature lover, spends as much time as possible hiking around the mountains and bird-watching near his home in Vancouver. So it pained him when he had to cut a big tree down in his front yard. Because he didn't want to disturb any of the animals living in it. But the tree was starting to rot, and he was afraid it would fall onto his house.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>这个故事始于一点点温柔。Alan Martin是个真正的自然爱好者，花尽可能多的时间在温哥华附近的群山中徒步和观鸟。所以当他不得不砍掉前院的一棵大树时，他非常痛苦。因为他不想打扰住在树上的任何动物。但那棵树开始腐烂，他担心它会倒在他的房子上。

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:55.850
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And so like the sensitive environmental scientist that he is, Alan waited until autumn, when he knew that nesting season was over. And then, just to be extra careful, he brought in an arborist to assess the tree to make sure he wouldn't be messing with any little critters' homes. He got the OK and hired a couple guys to cut the tree down while he stood by, directing them this way and that. And then, the next morning, Alan stepped out of his house, and--
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>作为一个敏感的环境科学家，Alan等到秋天，他知道筑巢季节已经结束时。然后，为了格外小心，他请了一位树艺师来评估这棵树，确保不会破坏任何小生物的家园。他得到了许可，雇了几个伙计砍树，他站在旁边指挥着他们。第二天早上，Alan走出屋子——

00:27:55.850 --> 00:28:03.890
<v Alan Martin>There was this crow sitting in the front lawn, and at a close distance to the steps. And it's just staring right at me. [CHUCKLES]
<v Alan Martin>那有一只乌鸦坐在前草坪上，距离台阶很近。它正盯着我看。[轻笑]

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:05.600
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>That's very weird.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>那很奇怪。

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:16.040
<v Alan Martin>It was a bit off-putting. I couldn't quite figure it out, but I walked down my path. And as soon as the crow was out of my sight, that's when I got this thump on the back of my head.
<v Alan Martin>让人有点不安。我不太明白，但我走下小路。就在乌鸦消失在视线之外时，我的后脑勺挨了一下。

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:25.550
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Out of the corner of his eye, Alan saw the crow fly past him. And he thought, did that crow just hit me? It hurt.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Alan用眼角余光看到乌鸦从他身边飞过。他想，那只乌鸦刚刚撞了我吗？很疼。

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:43.810
<v Alan Martin>And then I did a few little experiments. I would turn away purposely and walk away, and it would leave the power line, attending another hit. And I would turn around just in time, and it would swoop away. And I did this a couple of times. It was just clearly waiting for me to turn away, so that it could hit me in the back of the head.
<v Alan Martin>然后我做了一些小实验。我故意转身走开，它就离开了电线，发动另一次攻击。我及时转过身，它就俯冲走开了。我这样做了几次。它显然在等我转身，好撞击我的后脑勺。

00:28:43.810 --> 00:29:02.300
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Alan thought, OK, maybe this crow didn't live in this tree, but it must have meant something to it. He knew that crows are really smart, but they're also very vengeful birds and territorial. And so a short while later, when he walked out of his house--
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Alan想，好吧，也许这只乌鸦不住在树里，但它对这棵树一定有什么感情。他知道乌鸦很聪明，但也非常记仇且具有领地意识。所以不久之后，当他走出家门——

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:07.760
<v Alan Martin>I came out, and there was a crow sitting on the line. But it was a different crow.
<v Alan Martin>我出来时，有一只乌鸦坐在电线上。但那是一只不同的乌鸦。

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:08.950
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>How could you tell?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你怎么看出来的？

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:14.300
<v Alan Martin>It-- it was smaller. It was a bit smaller, and it wasn't as bulky.
<v Alan Martin>它——它比较小。体型小一点，没那么臃肿。

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:14.750
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>OK.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>好的。

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:33.750
<v Alan Martin>This crow, when I came outside, it caught at me, as if it almost recognized me, as if, oh, that's the guy. It cawed, and then it left. It flew down the block. And there's a green space at the end of the street. And about a minute later, it came back with the enforcer crow.
<v Alan Martin>我出来时，这只乌鸦对着我叫，仿佛它几乎认出我了，仿佛，“噢，就是那个人”。它叫了一声，然后离开了。它飞向街区另一头。街尽头有一块绿地。大约一分钟后，它带着那只执法者乌鸦回来了。

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:58.820
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>This smaller crow, snitching on Alan to the bigger crow-- this is a real thing. I ran Alan's story by a crow researcher named Loma Pendergraft. Loma said that if a crow sees something it deems dangerous, it will sound an alarm to summon other crows. And if they agree there's a threat, they'll dive-bomb. This whole routine is called "mobbing."
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>这只小乌鸦，向大乌鸦告发Alan——这可是真事。我把Alan的故事告诉了一位叫Loma Pendergraft的乌鸦研究员。Loma说，如果乌鸦看到它认为危险的东西，它会发出警报召集其他乌鸦。如果它们一致认为存在威胁，它们就会俯冲攻击。这一整套例程被称为“聚众攻击”。

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:05.530
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Some people, attacked by a murder of crows-- they'd be angry. Not Alan or his family.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>有些人，如果被一群乌鸦攻击——肯定会很生气。但Alan和他的家人没有。

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:09.520
<v Alan Martin>We felt bad. Because we realized we'd hurt these crows.
<v Alan Martin>我们感觉很糟糕。因为我们意识到我们伤害了这些乌鸦。

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.730
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Hmm. Well, that's a very nice reaction.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>嗯。那真是非常好的反应。

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:37.350
<v Alan Martin>[CHUCKLES] Yeah, that was initially. But as the harassment persisted over the coming days, it became a source of anxiety, just going outside. So the compassion for the crow, I would say, waned over time a little bit because of that. And my next questions were, how long is this going to go on for?
<v Alan Martin>[轻笑] 是的，最初是那样。但随着接下来几天骚扰的持续，这变成了一种焦虑的来源，仅仅是走出门就觉得焦虑。所以对乌鸦的同情心，我想随着时间的推移减弱了一些。我的下一个问题是，这种情况还要持续多久？

00:30:37.350 --> 00:31:12.210
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Science actually has an answer to this question. It turns out crows can hold grudges for a very long time. Back in 2006, John Marzluff, who is a professor in wildlife science at the University of Washington-- he set up an experiment to test this. He put on this ugly, orange, ogre mask and walked around an area where he knew crows lived. And then, he captured a bunch of the crows while the rest of the flock looked on. Then he released them. The point was to distress the crows, but not hurt them.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>科学实际上对这个问题有答案。事实证明，乌鸦可以长时间记仇。早在2006年，华盛顿大学野生动物科学教授John Marzluff做了一个实验来测试这一点。他戴上一个丑陋的、橙色的食人魔面具，走在他知道乌鸦居住的区域。然后，他在其他同类观察的情况下抓了一堆乌鸦。然后释放了它们。目的是让乌鸦感到痛苦，而不是伤害它们。

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:49.770
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And then, for the next 17 years, every time he put on his ogre mask and walked around the place where he first captured the birds, the crows would swarm him. For years, each time he returned, more and more crows would join in. Because crows not only remember their enemies, but pass their grievances onto their crow children and grandchildren. Alan was not aware of all of that. So for weeks, he'd leave his house every day, hoping the birds had finally forgotten about him. No such luck. So he tried a new tactic.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>然后在接下来的17年里，每次他戴上食人魔面具出现在首次捕获鸟类的地方，乌鸦就会蜂拥而上围攻他。多年来，每当他回来时，就会有更多的乌鸦加入。因为乌鸦不仅记得它们的敌人，还会把怨恨传给它们的乌鸦子女和孙子辈。Alan并不了解这一切。所以几个星期以来，他每天出门都希望鸟儿们终于忘记他了。很遗憾并没有。于是他尝试了一种新战术。

00:31:49.770 --> 00:32:03.300
<v Alan Martin>I put on a wig and a beard, and the crow was looking hard. It was kind of turning its head a bit, side to side. You can tell it was trying to figure out who this was. But it didn't recognize me.
<v Alan Martin>我戴上了假发和胡子，乌鸦死死地盯着看。它歪着脑袋，左右看了看。你可以看出它在试图弄清楚这是谁。但它没认出我。

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:16.490
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Just a PSA. There are other ways to ward off an angry crow, should you not have a wig on hand. You can hide yourself under an umbrella so the crows can't see you. Or you can wear sunglasses backwards.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>顺便提个建议。如果你手头没有假发，还有其他方法可以抵御愤怒的乌鸦。你可以藏在伞下，这样乌鸦就看不见你了。或者你可以把墨镜反戴。

00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:34.730
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Apparently, crows understand that sunglasses go on your eyes. And they know that if you can see them, you can potentially hurt them. So they won't attack the back of your sunglassed head, because they think you have an extra set of eyes back there. Anyway, Alan's disguise-- it was working perfectly for him.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>显然，乌鸦明白墨镜是戴在眼睛上的。它们知道如果你能看到它们，你可能会伤害它们。所以它们不会攻击你反戴墨镜的后脑勺，因为它们认为你后面也长了眼睛。总之，Alan的伪装——对他来说非常有效。

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:43.820
<v Alan Martin>And then, I ripped off the beard and the wig, and the crow flipped. It flipped out.
<v Alan Martin>然后，我扯掉了胡子和假发，乌鸦崩溃了。它彻底崩溃了。

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:44.100
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Really?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>真的吗？

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:45.620
<v Alan Martin>It did a backflip.
<v Alan Martin>它做了一个后空翻。

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:47.780
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>What? Oh, it literally flipped out?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>什么？噢，它字面上崩溃了？

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:58.180
<v Alan Martin>It just literally-- it kind of flipped back, up into the air a little bit. But it was completely in shock and extremely pissed off. Like, how dare you?
<v Alan Martin>它只是字面上——它向后翻了一下，飞到空中一点点。但它完全震惊了，而且非常愤怒。仿佛，你竟敢这样？

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:03.910
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It sounds like you're developing a whole kind of relationship with these birds, almost.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>听起来你几乎和这些鸟建立了一种完整的关系。

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:18.310
<v Alan Martin>Yeah. It's a relationship of antagonism, and it's not a rewarding one.
<v Alan Martin>是的。这是一种敌对的关系，而且不是一种有益的关系。

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:28.660
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Alan decided he had to make amends with the crows. A real truce. But how do you do that? How do you make things right with a couple of birds?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Alan决定他必须补偿这些乌鸦。达成真正的休战。但该怎么做呢？你如何与几只鸟重归于好？

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:48.990
<v Alan Martin>If you could have just sat down with them and I could have apologized, and maybe they could have explained why those trees were so important, and I could have shown some understanding for them. And maybe we could have worked things out that way. But because we're unable to do that, I just thought food is maybe the next best thing.
<v Alan Martin>如果我能坐下来和它们道歉，也许它们能解释为什么那些树那么重要，我也能表达我的理解。也许我们可以那样解决问题。但因为我们没法交流，我就觉得食物可能是下一个最好的选择。

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:53.650
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Food, you know, the tool of every animal trainer that's ever lived.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>食物，你知道，有史以来每一个动物训练师的工具。

00:33:53.650 --> 00:34:09.120
<v Alan Martin>And so I found this kind of brass, gold-colored plate. It was shiny. And so I put some almonds on it, and I walked outside with it above my head, kind of, holding my arms straight above my head. And I laid it out on the lawn.
<v Alan Martin>于是我找到了一个铜的、金色的盘子。它很闪亮。我放了一些杏仁在上面，然后举着盘子走出屋子，把胳膊直直地举在头顶上方。我就把它放在草坪上。

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:21.040
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The crows caw at him loudly while he puts the plate down. He goes back inside. And a couple hours later, Alan sees the plate sitting there, empty. The almonds are gone.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>他放盘子时，乌鸦对着他大声叫。他回到屋里。几个小时后，Alan看到盘子放在那里，是空的。杏仁没了。

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:47.750
<v Alan Martin>I did that for another three days. And then, it was on the fourth day. I came outside with the plate, but they weren't cawing this time. They seemed more subdued, less aggressive. And I put the plate out, I went back inside, and that was the last time I saw them.
<v Alan Martin>我又做了三天。然后，到了第四天。我带着盘子出来时，它们这次没有尖叫。它们显得更温顺了，没那么有攻击性。我把盘子放下，回到屋里，那是我最后一次见到它们。

00:34:47.750 --> 00:35:06.560
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The crow researcher told me that it seemed like, since Alan had merely stressed the crows out and not actually hurt them, it might have been easier for them to forget why they were mad at him in the first place. Alan said the whole thing, having a couple of birds angry at him-- it kind of hurt his feelings.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>乌鸦研究员告诉我，似乎因为Alan仅仅是让乌鸦感到压力，并没有真正伤害它们，它们可能更容易忘记当初为什么要生他的气。Alan说整件事，被几只鸟生他的气——伤害了他的感情。

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:31.270
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's not like he's naive. He knows nature doesn't care about his feelings. It's just that he loves nature so much, and his intentions were good. He can't help but feel like it would have been nice if the crows had shown a little tenderness towards him.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>他并不是天真。他知道大自然不在乎他的感受。只是他太热爱自然了，而且他的本意是好的。他不禁觉得，如果乌鸦能对他表现出一点温柔，那该多好。

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:38.650
<v Ira Glass>Aviva DeKornfeld is a producer of our program.
<v Ira Glass>Aviva DeKornfeld是我们节目的制作人。

00:35:38.650 --> 00:35:54.460
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, You'll Spank Me Later For This. So in the show today about tenderness, we turn now to comedian Josh Johnson, who's been thinking a lot lately about when tenderness is called for, and when the opposite of tenderness may actually be the right way to go.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《你以后会因为这个打我》。在今天关于温柔的节目中，我们要转而介绍喜剧演员Josh Johnson，他最近一直在思考何时需要温柔，以及何时温柔的反面可能才是正确的方法。

00:35:54.460 --> 00:36:18.800
<v Josh Johnson>OK. This is like-- this is, like, a contentious thing. So just stick with me for a second. Because it's weird-- it's weird that people are weird about it. OK. So my friends are having kids, right? A lot of them are starting to have kids, so they're all having the conversation about if they're going to spank their kids, right?
<v Josh Johnson>好的。这是一个——这非常有争议。所以请听我说几秒钟。因为很奇怪——人们对这个问题的反应很奇怪。好的。我的朋友们开始生孩子了，对吧？他们很多人开始有孩子了，所以他们都在讨论是否要打孩子，对吧？

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:47.330
<v Josh Johnson>And people have very strong feelings either way. Some people think, like, oh, if it's rare and it's warranted, then a spanking here and there is not that bad. And then, some people are like, no, this is, like-- this is full-on abuse. This is, like, you're creating trauma and everything. And I'm not even telling anybody how to feel. Like, I know that I was spanked when I was a kid. And it made sense that I was spanked, because we were poor.
<v Josh Johnson>人们对此有强烈的不同感受。有些人认为，如果情况罕见且有正当理由，那么偶尔打一打也没那么坏。然后，有些人觉得，不，这完全是——这是彻头彻尾的虐待。你在制造创伤等等。我并不是要告诉任何人该怎么感受。我知道我小时候被打过。而且打我有道理，因为我们很穷。

