00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:49.910
<v Josh Johnson>And he's really, like, floating and everything. And this guy is like, letting him have it, right? Like, one after the other. This dude spanked him so much, he took a break. There was one point where he was holding him suspended in the air by his belt, but also like-- whew.
<v Josh Johnson>他真的在飘着什么的。然后这哥们在让他接受惩罚，对吧？一个接一个。这哥们打了他屁股那么多次，他还休息了一下。有一次他用腰带把他悬在半空中，但也像——呼。

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:56.620
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:43:56.620 --> 00:44:31.180
<v Josh Johnson>And then, after, like, the 36th lick, lets go to the belt. Dude drops to the ground. He gets up, and he sort of limps off. A lot of expletives and stuff, but it's kind of done. And this guy goes back into the bar we were just in. Because he wasn't watching the show. The show was in, like, a back room. This guy was just in the bar. And I had no idea, like, what the fight was about. Because, once again, we had already walked out, and it was already happening. But he walked back in, and I'm like-- I'm a curious duck. You know what I mean? Like--
<v Josh Johnson>然后，在第36次拍打后，放开腰带。那家伙掉到地上。他站起来，一瘸一拐地走开了。很多脏话什么的，但基本结束了。而这哥们回到了我们刚刚所在的酒吧。因为他没看演出。演出在后面的房间。这哥们就在酒吧里。我根本不知道他们在打什么架。因为，再说一次，我们已经走出来了，而且这事已经在发生了。但他走回去了，而我——我是个好奇的小鸭子。你明白我的意思吗？就像——

00:44:31.180 --> 00:44:33.300
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:44:33.300 --> 00:44:52.960
<v Josh Johnson>So I go back in after him, and I'm like, hey, man, are you OK? Which is a weird thing to ask him. But-- it's like, are you OK? What was all that about? And then, he turns to me and goes-- I look like a lot of dudes' dads.
<v Josh Johnson>所以我跟着他进去，我说，嘿，伙计，你没事吧？这问他很奇怪。但——就像，你没事吧？那是什么回事？然后他转身对我说——我看起来像很多人的爸爸。

00:44:52.960 --> 00:45:04.500
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:22.830
<v Josh Johnson>This dude was, like, 30. And he-- and he looked like he could be a dad, right? And so he's like, no, I just look like a lot of dudes' dads. And I was like, wow, how did you find that out? And he was like, oh, yeah. You know, after the fourth or fifth fight--
<v Josh Johnson>这哥们大约30岁。他——他看起来确实像个爸爸，对吧？所以他说，不，我只是看起来像很多人的爸爸。我说，哇，你是怎么发现的？他说，哦，是的。你知道，在第四或第五次打架后——

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:27.080
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:29.100
<v Josh Johnson>--you just start to put things together.
<v Josh Johnson>——你就会把事情拼凑起来。

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:35.750
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:45:35.750 --> 00:46:05.930
<v Josh Johnson>And he told me. He told me that sometimes he goes to the bar with his friends. And he's like, after a while, I can just tell, some guy starts looking at me. And then, he comes, and he starts jawing at me. And I can tell it's not about me, because it's-- I was just sitting here. I wasn't doing anything. And then he wants to fight. And he either swings at me first or he says, let's go outside. But this happens pretty often.
<v Josh Johnson>他告诉我。他告诉我有时他会和朋友去酒吧。他说，过了一会儿，我一眼就能看出来，有些家伙开始盯着我。然后他走过来，开始跟我叫板。我知道那不是关于我，因为它是——我就坐在这。我什么也没做。然后他想打架。他要么先朝我挥拳，要么说，我们出去。但这经常发生。

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:13.560
<v Josh Johnson>And I was like, wow, are you a dad, though? And he was like, yeah.
<v Josh Johnson>我说，哇，你真的是爸爸吗？他说，是啊。

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:17.170
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:46:17.170 --> 00:46:18.590
<v Josh Johnson>I was like, do you spank your kid?
<v Josh Johnson>我说，你会打你孩子屁股吗？

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:21.080
<v Josh Johnson>He was like, oh, no. No, I would never.
<v Josh Johnson>他说，哦，不。不，我绝不会。

00:46:21.080 --> 00:46:33.280
<v Josh Johnson>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Johnson>[笑声]

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:43.750
<v Ira Glass>Josh Johnson. Check out his YouTube channel, Josh Johnson Comedy. Tour dates, videos, and albums are at joshjohnsoncomedy.com.
<v Ira Glass>Josh Johnson。去看看他的YouTube频道Josh Johnson Comedy。巡演日期、视频和专辑都在joshjohnsoncomedy.com。

00:46:43.750 --> 00:47:02.350
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, The Feels on the Bus. There are, of course, so many people who believe that being tenderhearted is not a good way to live in this tough and unforgiving world. For some people, it's a matter of principle. They think it works out better for everybody that way. Etgar Keret has this short story about someone like that.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，《公交车上的情感》。当然，有很多人认为有一颗柔软的心并不是在这个艰难且无情的世界上生存的好方法。对一些人来说，这是一个原则问题。他们认为那样对每个人都更好。Etgar Keret有一个关于这样的人的短篇小说。

00:47:02.350 --> 00:47:24.010
<v Etgar Keret>This is a story about a bus driver who would never open the door of the bus for people who were late. Not for anyone. Not for repressed high school kids who'd run alongside the bus and stare at it longingly, and not for high-strung people in windbreakers who'd bang on the door as if they were actually on time, and it was the driver who was out of line.
<v Etgar Keret>这是一个关于公交车司机的故事，他从不为迟到的人打开公交车门。对谁都不。对那些跟着公交车跑、渴望地盯着它的压抑高中生，不对；对那些穿风衣、仿佛他们真的没迟到、是司机的问题而敲门的紧张人士，不对。

00:47:24.010 --> 00:47:42.000
<v Etgar Keret>And not even for little old ladies with brown paper bags full of groceries, who struggled to flag him down with trembling hands. And it wasn't because he was mean that he didn't open the door. Because this driver didn't have a mean bone in his body. It was a matter of ideology.
<v Etgar Keret>即使是对提着装满食品杂货的棕色纸袋、挣扎着挥动颤抖的手想拦下他的老太太也不行。他不开门并不是因为他刻薄。因为这个司机身上根本没有刻薄的骨头。这是一个意识形态问题。

00:47:42.000 --> 00:48:27.590
<v Etgar Keret>The driver's ideology said that even if the delay caused by opening the door for someone who came late, was just under 30 seconds, and even if not opening the door meant that this person would wind up losing 15 minutes of his life, it would still be more fair to society to not open the door. Because the seconds would be lost by every single passenger on the bus. And if there were, say, 60 people on the bus who hadn't done anything wrong and had all arrived to the bus stop on time, then together, they'd be losing half an hour, which is double 15 minutes.
<v Etgar Keret>司机的意识形态说，即使为迟到的人开门造成的延误只有不到30秒，即使不开门意味着这个人会损失15分钟的生命，不对人开门对社会依然更公平。因为这些秒数是车上每一位乘客损失的。如果有，比如说，车上有60个没做错什么而且都准时到达车站的人，那么加起来，他们会损失半个小时，是15分钟的两倍。

00:48:27.590 --> 00:49:05.950
<v Etgar Keret>This was the only reason why he never opened the door. He knew that the passengers hadn't the slightest idea what his reason was, and that the people running after the bus and signaling him to stop had no idea either. He also knew that most of them thought he was just an ass [BLEEP], and that personally, it would have been much, much easier for him to let them on and receive their smiles and thanks. Except that when it came to choosing between smiles and thanks on the one hand and the good of society on the other, this driver knew what it had to be.
<v Etgar Keret>这就是他从不开门的唯一原因。他知道乘客们一点都不知道他的理由是什么，那些追着公交车、示意他停车的人也不知道。他也知道他们中大多数人认为他只是个混蛋，而且个人而言，如果让他们上车并收到他们的微笑和感谢，对他来说会容易得多。只是当需要在微笑、感谢和社会利益之间做选择时，这个司机知道该怎么选。

00:49:05.950 --> 00:49:18.760
<v Etgar Keret>The person who should have suffered the most from the driver's ideology was named Eddie. But unlike the other people in this story, he wouldn't even try to run for the bus. That's how lazy and out of it he was.
<v Etgar Keret>本来应该在这个司机的意识形态中遭受损失最大的人叫Eddie。但不同于这个故事中的其他人，他甚至不会试着跑去追公交车。他就是那么懒，那么不在状态。

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:30.710
<v Etgar Keret>Now, Eddie was assistant cook at a restaurant called The Steakout, which was the best pun that the stupid owner of this place could come up with. The food there was nothing to write home about.
<v Etgar Keret>现在，Eddie是一家叫“The Steakout”的餐厅的副厨师，这是那个愚蠢的老板能想出的最好的双关语。那里的食物实在是不敢恭维。

00:49:30.710 --> 00:49:56.290
<v Etgar Keret>But Eddie himself was a really nice guy. So nice, that sometimes when something he made didn't come out too great, he'd serve it to the table himself and apologize. It was during one of these apologies that he met happiness, or at least a shot at happiness, in the form of a girl that was so sweet that she tried to finish the entire portion of roast beef he brought her, just so he wouldn't feel bad.
<v Etgar Keret>但Eddie本人是个非常好的人。好到有时他做的一些菜做得不太好，他会自己端到桌前道歉。就在其中一次道歉中，他遇到了幸福，或者说至少是一次得到幸福的机会，形式是一个女孩，她太甜了，为了不让他感到难过，她试图吃完他端给她的整份烤牛肉。

00:49:56.290 --> 00:50:18.100
<v Etgar Keret>And this girl didn't want to tell him her name or give him her phone number. But she was sweet enough to agree to meet him the next day at 5:00 at a spot they decided on together. The Dolphinarium, to be exact.
<v Etgar Keret>这个女孩不想告诉他她的名字，也不想给他电话号码。但她甜到同意第二天5点在他们商定的地方见他。确切地说，是海豚馆。

00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:34.330
<v Etgar Keret>Now, Eddie had this condition, one that had already caused him to miss out on all sorts of things in life. It wasn't one of those conditions where your adenoids get all swollen or anything like that. But still, it had already caused him a lot of damage.
<v Etgar Keret>现在，Eddie有这种毛病，这种毛病已经让他错过了生活中各种各样的事情。它不是那种腺样体肿大什么的毛病。但依然，它已经给他造成了很多伤害。

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:53.950
<v Etgar Keret>This sickness always made him oversleep by 10 minutes, and no alarm clock did any good. That was why he was always late for work at The Steakout. That, and our bus driver, the one who always chose the good of society over smiles and thanks.
<v Etgar Keret>这种病总是让他睡过头10分钟，什么闹钟都没用。这就是他为什么总是上班迟到。那个原因，加上我们的公交车司机，总是选择社会利益而不是微笑和感谢的那位。

00:50:53.950 --> 00:51:27.540
<v Etgar Keret>Except that this time, since happiness was at stake, Eddie decided to beat the condition. And instead of taking an afternoon nap, he stayed awake and watched television. Just to be on the safe side, he even lined up, not one, but three alarm clocks, and ordered the wakeup call to boot. But this sickness was incurable, and Eddie fell asleep like a baby, watching the kiddy channel.
<v Etgar Keret>只不过这次，因为幸福危在旦夕，Eddie决定战胜这个毛病。他没有午睡，而是保持清醒看电视。为了保险起见，他甚至排成一排，不止是一个，而是三个闹钟，还预约了叫醒电话。但这病是绝症，Eddie像个婴儿一样睡着了，看着儿童频道。

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:45.240
<v Etgar Keret>He woke up in a sweat to the screeching of a trillion, million alarm clocks. 10 minutes too late. Rushed out of the house without stopping to change and ran toward the bus stop. He barely remembered how to run anymore, and his feet fumbled a bit every time they left the sidewalk.
<v Etgar Keret>他在一万亿个闹钟的尖叫声中惊醒。迟了10分钟。冲出房子，没来得及换衣服就向公交车站跑去。他几乎不记得怎么跑步了，每次离开人行道他的脚都会踉跄一下。

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:58.260
<v Etgar Keret>The last time he ran was before he discovered he could cut gym class, which was about in the sixth grade. Except that unlike in those gym classes, this time, he ran like crazy. Because now, he had something to lose.
<v Etgar Keret>他上次跑步是在他发现可以逃掉体育课之前，大概是在六年级。只不过不同于那些体育课，这次他疯狂地跑。因为现在，他有东西可输。

00:51:58.260 --> 00:52:15.920
<v Etgar Keret>And all the pain in his chest and his Lucky Strike wheezing weren't going to get in the way of his pursuit of happiness. Nothing was going to get in his way. Except our bus driver, who had just closed the door and was beginning to pull away.
<v Etgar Keret>胸部的剧痛和Lucky Strike烟带来的喘鸣无法阻挡他对幸福的追求。没有什么能阻挡他。除了我们的公交车司机，他刚刚关上门，开始驶离。

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:47.600
<v Etgar Keret>The driver saw Eddie in the rearview mirror. But as we've already explained, he had an ideology that, more than anything, relied on love of justice and on simple arithmetic. Except that Eddie didn't care about the driver's arithmetic. For the first time in his life, he really wanted to get somewhere on time. And that's why he went right on chasing the bus, even though he didn't have a chance.
<v Etgar Keret>司机在后视镜里看到了Eddie。但正如我们已经解释过的，他有一个意识形态，比起其他任何东西，更依赖于对正义的爱和简单的算术。只不过Eddie不在乎司机的算术。这是他一生中第一次真正想准时到达某个地方。这就是为什么他继续追着公交车，尽管他没什么机会。

00:52:47.600 --> 00:53:01.030
<v Etgar Keret>But then, Eddie's luck turned. But only halfway. 100 yards past the bus stop, there was a traffic light. And just a second before the bus reached it, the traffic light turned red.
<v Etgar Keret>但随后，Eddie的运气转了。但只转了一半。离公交车站100码的地方有一个交通灯。就在公交车到达的前一秒，交通灯变红了。

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:16.060
<v Etgar Keret>Eddie managed to catch up with the bus and to drag himself all the way to the driver's door. He didn't even bang on the glass, he was so weak. He just looked at the driver with moist eyes and fell to his knees, panting and wheezing.
<v Etgar Keret>Eddie勉强追上了公交车，拖着身子一直走到司机车门边。他甚至没敲玻璃，他太虚弱了。他只是湿润着眼睛看着司机，瘫跪在地上，喘着气，呼哧作响。

00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:38.860
<v Etgar Keret>And this reminded the driver of something. Something from out of the past, from a time before he wanted to become a bus driver, when he still wanted to become God. It was kind of a sad memory, because the driver didn't become God at the end. But it was a happy one, too, because he became a bus driver, which was his second choice.
<v Etgar Keret>这让司机想起了一些事。过去的一些事，在他想成为公交车司机之前，当他仍然想成为上帝的时候。那是一种悲伤的记忆，因为司机最终没能成为上帝。但这也是一种幸福的记忆，因为他成为了公交车司机，这是他的第二选择。

00:53:38.860 --> 00:54:18.360
<v Etgar Keret>And suddenly, the driver remembered how he at once promised himself that if he became God in the end, he'd be merciful and kind and would listen to all his creatures. So when he saw Eddie from way up in his driver's seat, kneeling on the asphalt, he simply couldn't go through with it. And in spite of all his ideology and his simple arithmetic, he opened the door, and Eddie got on and didn't even say "thank you," he was so out of breath.
<v Etgar Keret>突然，司机想起他曾答应过自己，如果最终成为了上帝，他会仁慈、善良，倾听所有生物的声音。所以当他从高高的驾驶座上看到Eddie，跪在沥青上，他简直无法坚持下去。尽管有他所有的意识形态和简单的算术，他打开了门，Eddie上了车，甚至没说“谢谢”，他实在喘不过气来。

00:54:18.360 --> 00:54:30.720
<v Etgar Keret>OK. Maybe the best thing would be to stop listening right now. Because even though Eddie did get to the Dolphinarium on time, happiness didn't come. Because happiness already had a boyfriend.
<v Etgar Keret>好的。也许最好现在就停止收听。因为尽管Eddie确实准时到了海豚馆，幸福并没有来。因为幸福已经有了男朋友。

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:46.320
<v Etgar Keret>It's just that she was so sweet that she couldn't bring herself to tell Eddie, so she preferred to stand him up. Eddie waited for her on the bench they'd agreed on for almost two hours. While he sat there, he kept thinking all sorts of depressing thoughts about his life.
<v Etgar Keret>只不过她太甜了，以至于无法忍心告诉Eddie，所以她宁愿放他鸽子。Eddie在他们约定好的长椅上等了她近两个小时。他坐在那儿时，一直在想关于他生活的各种沮丧念头。

00:54:46.320 --> 00:55:09.230
<v Etgar Keret>And while he was at it, he watched the sunset, which was a pretty good one, and thought about how cramped his legs were going to be later on. When he was really desperate to get home, he saw his bus in the distance, pulling in at the bus stop and letting go of passengers. And he knew that even if he'd had the strength to run, he'd never catch up with it anyway.
<v Etgar Keret>与此同时，他看着日落，那是一个相当不错的日落，还想到他的腿之后会有多酸痛。当他真的很渴望回家时，他在远处看到了他的公交车，停在车站让乘客下车。他知道即使他有力气跑，也永远追不上了。

00:55:09.230 --> 00:55:53.390
<v Etgar Keret>So he just kept on walking slowly, feeling about a million tired muscles with every step. And when he finally reached the bus stop, he saw that the bus was still there, waiting for him. And even though the passengers were shouting and grumbling to get a move on, the driver waited for Eddie, and he didn't touch the accelerator till Eddie was seated. And when they started moving, he looked in the rearview mirror and gave Eddie a sad wink, which somehow made the whole thing almost bearable.
<v Etgar Keret>所以他就一直慢慢地走着，每走一步都能感觉到一百万个疲惫的肌肉。当他最终到达公交车站时，他看到公交车还在那儿，等着他。尽管乘客们在叫嚷、抱怨着快点开，司机还是等了Eddie，直到Eddie坐下他才碰加速踏板。当他们开始移动时，他看着后视镜，给了Eddie一个悲伤的眨眼，这莫名其妙地让整件事变得几乎可以忍受。

00:55:53.390 --> 00:56:44.610
<v Ira Glass>Etgar Keret, reading the title story from his collection, The Bus Driver Who Wanted to be God, and Other Stories. If you want to get a short story in your email from Etgar once a week, you can sign up for that at his Substack, at [email protected] that's Keret, K-E-R-E-T.
